    - **Late** (red)

- **Time encoding**
  - The alpha-shape boundaries are additionally colored by a continuous color scale labeled **“Hours since Mw 6.4 mainshock”**, ranging from about **0 to 33 hours**.
  - The scale progresses from **purple/blue (earliest)** through **green/yellow** to **orange/red (latest)**.

- **Major geometric patterns of alpha-shape evolution**
  - The evolving boundaries define a **narrow, NW–SE-trending corridor** of seismicity/occupied area.
  - A **southern-central linear strand** extends from roughly **(−117.60, 35.56)** toward **(−117.50, 35.70)**.
  - Near **35.69–35.71 latitude**, the pattern becomes more complex, with **branching and clustered outlines**.
  - A **northwestern cluster** is centered near the **Mw 7.1 star** around **(−117.60, 35.78)**.
  - The central area near the **Mw 6.4 epicenter** also shows dense, repeatedly overlapping outlines, indicating persistent occupation there.

- **Evidence for focusing / defocusing / bifurcation / migration**
  - **Migration toward the Mw 7.1 area:** Yes. The early-to-late sequence appears to progress from the **southern and central linear zone** toward the **northwestern cluster** near the **Mw 7.1 epicenter**, consistent with spatial expansion or migration in that direction.
  - **Focusing:** There is apparent **focusing/localization** along the central linear strand and especially into the **northwestern node** near Mw 7.1, where many outlines overlap in a compact area.
  - **Defocusing:** Some **broadening** is visible in the central section near **35.68–35.71**, where outlines spread laterally rather than remaining on a single narrow line.
  - **Bifurcation:** The geometry shows a **branching/bifurcating structure** in the central-northern part of the sequence, with one trend continuing NW toward the Mw 7.1 area and another set of outlines extending more easterly around the Mw 6.4 region.

Overall, the figure shows hourly alpha-shape boundaries tracking an inter-mainshock seismicity field that is **initially elongated along a central fault-aligned corridor, then broadens and branches, with later activity increasingly concentrated toward the northwestern Mw 7.1 epicentral area**.[0m

